---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Workspaces from '../components/Workspaces.astro';
---

<Base
  title="namesync — terminal workspaces named after what you’re doing"
  description="A herdr plugin that keeps workspace, tab and agent names in sync with the intent of the coding agent running inside them. No LLM call, no API key, no polling."
  section="home"
>
  <main>
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="hero__copy">
        <h1 class="hero__title t-display">
          The agent knows what it is working on.
          <em>Your sidebar doesn’t.</em>
        </h1>
        <p class="hero__lede t-lead">
          Spaces get named after directories, because that is all herdr knew when you
          opened them. Meanwhile the agent inside each one has been describing its own
          work the entire time. namesync moves that name onto the space, and leaves the
          ones you named yourself alone.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="hero__demo">
        <Workspaces />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="band__inner prose">
        <h2 class="band__heading">Nothing generates the name</h2>
        <p>
          Every other renamer calls a model: your first prompt goes to an on-device
          summariser, or to Codex, or to whatever your <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code> points at.
        </p>
        <p>
          That work is already done. Claude Code, Codex and the rest publish a running
          summary of the current task as their terminal title, and herdr already captures
          it. Ask it yourself:
        </p>
        <pre><code>$ herdr agent list | jq -r '.result.agents[].terminal_title_stripped'
Astro docs site GNU style
Richard Stallman perspective
Open source wifi e-reader</code></pre>
        <p>
          So namesync spends its complexity somewhere else: on deciding <em>when</em> a
          name should change, and on getting it onto every surface that shows one.
          No API key, no token cost, no polling loop.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--rule">
      <div class="band__inner prose">
        <h2 class="band__heading">When it renames</h2>
        <p>
          herdr emits an event when a pane’s title changes, and deliberately not when
          only the spinner moved. That event is the trigger. Everything after it is the
          part that decides whether to act.
        </p>

        <dl class="rules">
          <div class="rules__row">
            <dt>Your names win</dt>
            <dd>
              A label that isn’t the one namesync last wrote was written by you. It gets
              locked and is never touched again.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="rules__row">
            <dt>Defaults are fair game</dt>
            <dd>
              <code>bedreader</code>, <code>w3</code>, <code>tab 2</code>, empty. Nobody
              chose these, so namesync claims them.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="rules__row">
            <dt>Rewording isn’t new intent</dt>
            <dd>
              “naming plugin” to “naming plugins” scores 1.00 on a stemmed token overlap
              and is skipped, so the sidebar doesn’t flicker.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="rules__row">
            <dt>It waits for you to settle</dt>
            <dd>
              Titles churn early in a turn. A rename holds for a debounce window, then
              at most once per workspace per interval.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="rules__row">
            <dt>It stays quiet when you’re stuck</dt>
            <dd>
              While an agent waits on an approval dialog its title describes the
              question, not the work. namesync sits that out and retries.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="rules__row">
            <dt>It won’t guess</dt>
            <dd>
              A workspace holding two agents has no single intent, so its tabs get named
              instead and the space is left alone.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <p>
          <code>namesync dry-run</code> prints every decision and the reason behind it,
          and changes nothing. It is the right place to start.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--rule">
      <div class="band__inner prose">
        <h2 class="band__heading">Install</h2>
        <pre><code>herdr plugin install oddurs/namesync
herdr integration install claude</code></pre>
        <p>
          herdr 0.8 or newer, Node 18 or newer, no npm dependencies. The startup hook
          launches a watcher that holds one event subscription and reconnects on its own
          across herdr restarts.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/docs/install">Read the docs</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  main { padding: 0 var(--gutter); }

  .hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
    align-items: center;
    max-width: var(--page-wide);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5.5rem) 0 clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem);
  }

  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .hero { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.05fr) minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }

  /* The italic is the rhetorical turn of the sentence, not an accent on a
     word: the first clause states, the second undercuts it. */
  /* Type comes from .t-display; this only positions it. The negative margin
     pulls the cap of the T back to the optical margin so the headline aligns
     with the lede below it. */
  .hero__title {
    margin: 0 0 0.62em -0.028em;
  }

  /* The turn in the sentence. IBM Plex Sans has a true italic — drawn, not
     slanted — so it reads as a change of voice rather than a tilt. */
  .hero__title em {
    display: block;
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: var(--weight-regular);
    letter-spacing: -0.019em;
    color: var(--fg-strong);
  }

  .hero__lede {
    max-width: 27rem;
  }

  .band { padding: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 4.75rem) 0; }
  .band--rule { border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }

  /* One left spine for the whole page: the reading column starts at the same
     edge as the headline instead of being centred, so the eye never has to
     re-find where the text begins. */
  .band__inner {
    max-width: var(--page-wide);
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .band__inner > * {
    max-width: min(var(--measure), 100%);
  }

  /* These sections already sit behind a full-width rule, so the heading drops
     the one the prose styles add. */
  .band__heading {
    margin-top: 0;
    padding-top: 0;
    border-top: 0;
  }

  .rules { margin: 1.8em 0; }

  .rules__row {
    padding: 1.05em 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .rules__row:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }

  /* Label in sans, description in serif: the same split as the rest of the
     site, at the scale of one row. */
  .rules dt {
    font-weight: var(--weight-semi);
    color: var(--fg-strong);
    margin-bottom: 0.3em;
  }

  .rules dd { color: var(--fg-muted); }

  @media (min-width: 44rem) {
    .rules__row {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 12rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-5);
      align-items: baseline;
    }

    .rules dt { margin-bottom: 0; }
  }
</style>
